Coordinates
coordFlip() turns the plot sideways while the mappings stay as written. This is the usual way to give long category labels room.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'manufacturer', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;boxplot(fill: '#C6DBEF')
    -&gt;coordFlip()
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Highway mpg');

coordCartesian(xlim:, ylim:), xlim(), and ylim() fix the domains and clip marks outside them. ggplot2’s xlim() removes the data outside the limits, which changes statistics such as smoothers. phiz keeps the data and only zooms.
coordFixed(ratio) fixes the aspect ratio, as coord_fixed() does: one unit of y takes ratio times the length of one unit of x. The panel’s height follows from its width (300 pixels unless set) and the data ranges, and the scales keep those ranges exactly. coord_equal() is the same with a ratio of 1.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'cty', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.5)
    -&gt;abline(color: 'gray', linetype: 'dashed')
    -&gt;coordFixed()
    -&gt;labs(x: 'City mpg', y: 'Highway mpg');

coordPolar() draws bar() and col() layers in polar coordinates. theta: 'y' stacks the bars into a pie (leave x unmapped, or map it to a constant), and innerRadius: in pixels makes a donut. theta: 'x' makes a rose: one equal slice per level of x, with radius y. start: rotates the first slice clockwise from twelve o’clock, in radians, and direction: -1 runs anticlockwise. Other geoms have no polar form in Vega-Lite, and phiz rejects them.
use Phiz\Compose;
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$cars = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg());

$plot = Compose::beside(
    $cars-&gt;aes(fill: 'class')-&gt;bar()-&gt;coordPolar(theta: 'y')-&gt;title('Pie'),
    $cars-&gt;aes(x: 'class', fill: 'class')-&gt;bar()-&gt;coordPolar()-&gt;title('Rose'),
)-&gt;resolve(['scale' =&gt; ['theta' =&gt; 'independent', 'radius' =&gt; 'independent']]);

Livewire
&lt;x-phiz&gt; redraws inside Livewire without extra code: each render updates the chart’s specification, and the loader redraws the chart when it changes. When a component renders several charts, give each a wire:key.
class RentChart extends Component
{
    public int $bedrooms = 2;

    public function render()
    {
        $rentals = Rental::where('bedrooms', $this-&gt;bedrooms)
            -&gt;get();

        $plot = Plot::of($rentals)
            -&gt;aes(x: 'area_m2', y: 'rent', color: 'neighborhood')
            -&gt;point(alpha: 0.6)
            -&gt;smooth(method: 'lm', se: false);

        return view('livewire.rent-chart', ['plot' =&gt; $plot]);
    }
}
&lt;div&gt;
    &lt;select wire:model.live="bedrooms"&gt;...&lt;/select&gt;
    &lt;x-phiz
        :plot="$plot"
        wire:key="rents"
        :options="['theme' =&gt; 'auto']"
    /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
'theme' =&gt; 'auto' follows the reader’s light or dark mode. Dashboards with Livewire builds components with one chart and with several linked charts.

A first chart
Plot::of() takes the data, aes() maps fields to visual channels, and a geom such as point() adds a layer. Datasets provides the classic datasets used in these pages.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point();

$plot-&gt;save('chart.html') writes a page that draws the chart in a browser. The page loads vega, vega-lite, and vega-embed from the jsDelivr CDN, so it needs a network connection. Image formats need vl-convert (Save a chart to a file).

Mappings and fixed properties
Mapping another field to color gives each class its own color and adds a legend. Fixed properties, such as a point size or an opacity, are geom arguments.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'class')
    -&gt;point(size: 40, alpha: 0.7);

Layers
Each geom adds a layer, and layers inherit the plot’s mappings.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.4)
    -&gt;smooth(span: 0.6)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg');

Plots are immutable. Every method returns a new plot, so a base plot can be stored and extended in several directions.
$mpg = Datasets::mpg();
$base = Plot::of($mpg)-&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy');

$scatter = $base-&gt;point();
$smooth = $base-&gt;point(alpha: 0.3)-&gt;smooth(method: 'lm');

Pipes
PHP 8.5’s pipe operator composes ggplot2-style functions. ggplot() starts a plot, and each geom_*(), scale_*(), labs(), or theme_*() function returns a closure that takes a plot and returns the next one. The method chain builds the same specification on every supported PHP version.

PHP 8.5+PHP 8.3–8.4


use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\{aes, geom_point, geom_smooth, ggplot, labs, theme_minimal};

$plot = ggplot(Datasets::mpg(), aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'drv')) |&gt;
    geom_point(alpha: 0.5) |&gt;
    geom_smooth(method: 'lm', se: false) |&gt;
    labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: 'Drive') |&gt;
    theme_minimal();


use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\aes;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg(), aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'drv'))
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.5)
    -&gt;smooth(method: 'lm', se: false)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: 'Drive')
    -&gt;theme('minimal');




The functions and the methods take the same arguments. On PHP 8.3 and 8.4, apply() takes the functions instead of the pipe, and it also applies reusable fragments:
$house = fn (Plot $plot) =&gt; $plot
    -&gt;theme('minimal', baseSize: 12)
    -&gt;size(480, 300);

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point()
    -&gt;apply($house);

Statistics

Coefficient plot
From a fit in the shape phit’s fit_lm() returns.
Vega-Lite specification
coefplot($fit)
    -&gt;labs(title: 'mpg ~ wt + hp + am');


Comparing models
Several fits, dodged.
Vega-Lite specification
coefplot([
    'Full' =&gt; $fit,
    'Without am' =&gt; $reduced,
]);


Regression diagnostics
Residuals, QQ, scale-location, and observed against fitted.
Vega-Lite specification
diagnostic_plots($fit, width: 290, height: 190);


Predicted values
Predicted mpg with 95% confidence bands as weight varies, by transmission.
Vega-Lite specification
effect_plot(
    $fit,
    $mtcars,
    'wt',
    by: 'am',
    design: fn ($r) =&gt; [1, $r['wt'], $r['hp'], $r['am']],
)
    -&gt;labs(
        x: 'Weight (1000 lbs)',
        y: 'mpg',
        color: 'Manual',
        fill: 'Manual',
    );


Posterior intervals
From CmdStanPHP draws_columns(), as bayesplot’s mcmc_intervals() draws them.
Vega-Lite specification
interval_plot($draws);


Means with error bars
Precomputed means with normal-approximation 95% intervals.
Vega-Lite specification
Plot::of(array_map(
    function ($class) use ($mpg) {
        $rows = array_filter(
            $mpg,
            fn ($r) =&gt; $r['class'] === $class,
        );
        $values = array_column($rows, 'hwy');
        $n = count($values);
        $mean = array_sum($values) / $n;
        $squares = array_map(
            fn ($v) =&gt; ($v - $mean) ** 2,
            $values,
        );
        $se = sqrt(array_sum($squares) / ($n - 1) / $n);

        return [
            'class' =&gt; $class,
            'mean' =&gt; $mean,
            'lower' =&gt; $mean - 1.96 * $se,
            'upper' =&gt; $mean + 1.96 * $se,
        ];
    },
    array_values(array_unique(array_column($mpg, 'class'))),
))
    -&gt;aes(x: 'class', y: 'mean', ymin: 'lower', ymax: 'upper')
    -&gt;errorbar(width: 8)
    -&gt;point(size: 50)
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Mean highway mpg (95% CI)');


Summaries
mean_cl_normal draws means with t-based 95% intervals over the raw data.
Vega-Lite specification
Plot::of($mpg)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'class', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.2)
    -&gt;summary(funData: 'mean_cl_normal', color: '#D62728')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Highway mpg');


Functions
curve() draws a PHP function, here the normal density over a histogram.
Vega-Lite specification
Plot::of(array_map(
    fn ($i) =&gt; [
        'z' =&gt; Fit::normalQuantile(($i + 0.5) / 500),
    ],
    range(0, 499),
))
    -&gt;aes(x: 'z')
    -&gt;histogram(
        binwidth: 0.25,
        density: true,
        fill: '#DDDDDD',
    )
    -&gt;curve(
        fn (float $x) =&gt; exp(-$x * $x / 2) / sqrt(2 * M_PI),
        -3.5,
        3.5,
        aes: aes(x: 'z', y: 'density'),
        color: '#D62728',
        linewidth: 2,
    );

